From 67805096de50eee7ea6a6b5e4bebfe45f7f15c64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: forklift <forklift@localhost>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:16:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] forklift: live-id loops, the deletion contract, anchor by
 id, and C (delete older than T)

With a deleting feature (C now, S1 later) state ids get holes: FindPossibleLoopClosure and
FindNearChains bound their id loops by one past the largest live id instead of size().
A node is deleted in the lifelong order -- RemoveNodeFromGraph, the sensor manager's maps,
the dataset (its owner frees the scan; SlamToolbox registers itself as the remover), then
the vertex -- under smapper_mutex_. The Ceres solver holds the fixed node by id and looks
it up when used; when the fixed node is deleted the oldest live node becomes fixed at the
solver's current value. C: after each processed keyframe the nodes older than
FORKLIFT_SLAM_DELETE_OLDER_S sim seconds are deleted in id order, sparing the running
scans, the last scan and the new scan. All of it is off unless a deleting feature is on.
When deleting, the id loops step over live ids by map lookup (lower/upper_bound), so their cost follows the
live nodes rather than every id ever made. The dataset remover is registered only with a deleting feature on and
has one owner: a second node in one process is refused (exit 3), and clearing works only for the owner.
gtests: C's victims, the id bound, the live-id steps, the one-owner remover, the new anchor.
